  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 145 PM CDT Sun Sep 13 2026\n\nTonight: Clear and Cold\n\nA 1022-mb surface high pressure cell is sliding out of North \nDakota and into Minnesota this afternoon and continues its \neastward march overnight. Clear skies, light winds, and drier \ncontinental air should result in favorable conditions for some \nof the coldest temperatures we've seen thus far this \nmeteorological fall. Upstream temperatures fell into the lower \n30s along the North Dakota/Canadian border and similar \ntemperatures are expected over northern Wisconsin tonight. MOS \nguidance is targeting the sandy bogs of Jackson and Monroe \ncounty for falling into the lower 30s as well, so have issued a\nFrost Advisory for these two counties tonight. \n\n\nMonday Afternoon - Tuesday Morning: Moderate to Heavy Rain\n\nIncreasing mid-tropospheric theta-e advection begins in earnest \nMonday morning in the immediate passage of the mid to upper \nlevel ridge axis, but it will take until later in the afternoon \nfor the surface pattern to catch up. This has resulted in a \nslightly later forecast arrival time of the rainfall Monday, but\nthe rain should reach most locales before the late afternoon. \nThe focus for heavier rainfall amounts remains on Monday \nevening into early Tuesday morning as the deeper lower \ntropospheric moisture arrives and warm cloud depths/PWATs \nsupport efficient rainfall processes. \n\nHowever, the surface cold front will be steadily make its way \nfrom northwest to southeast during this period, keeping the axis\nof heavy rain fluid in space and possibly limiting the overall \nimpacts from this event. There is still a modest signal (30-50%)\nfor 1-2 inches of rain for much of the forecast area, but any \npockets of higher amounts will be more mesoscale driven and \nlikely not resolved until the event is ongoing. By Tuesday \nmorning, the front should be on the verge of exiting the \nforecast area and taking the rainfall with it.\n\n\nMid to Late Week: Seasonal Temperatures, Periodic Rain\n\nBroad subtropical ridging remains in force across the southern \nand southeastern U.S. through the week. This will act to keep \nthe lower tropospheric baroclinic zone that brought our early \nweek rainfall from sinking too far south--likely only as far as \nthe lower Missouri River Valley by Tuesday night. A northern \nstream wave passing along the U.S./Canadian border may act to \npull some of this moisture northward, though how far northward \nremains a source of uncertainty in the medium range solutions. \n\nShould the front shift to the northern side of the solution \nenvelope, we would need to monitor the potential for more heavy \nrain, mainly over northeastern Iowa into southwestern Wisconsin.\nThis idea is reflected well in the latest EC EFI where the \nactual EFI values are less than 50%, but there is a notable \nshift of tails indicating that those outlier members have \nrespectively high rainfall amounts (in excess of 3 inches). \n\nThe synoptic pattern is progged to amplify slightly at the end \nof the week as a cutoff low over the western CONUS begins to \nmigrate eastward. How fast this trough advances remains poorly \nresolved and the aforementioned uncertainties in the northern \nextent of the baroclinic zone after midweek makes the \nprecipitation forecast beyond Wednesday one of lower confidence.\nWhat we can say with higher certainty is that temperatures \nshould remain in the 60s and 70s for highs, a solid taste of \nlate summer/early fall weather.",
